  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>You can stay in Cannes with your dog, but the rules depend on each hotel: maximum size, supplement, rooms concerned. This page brings together what the official sources read allow us to say, without listing any hotel whose policy has not been read on its own website.</p>
      <h2>A hotel that publishes its rule</h2>
      <p>The Cannes Motel, at 245 avenue Francis Tonner in Cannes La Bocca, publishes its dog policy on its website: pets under 10 kg stay free of charge, and for a pet of 10 kg or more you must contact reception before booking. The hotel recommends the Superior twin room with private terrace (2 people, two 90×200 cm beds) and asks guests to declare the pet before the stay. The private car park is free and lets you park close to your room. The website does not say whether dogs can access the pool or the restaurant: reception can tell you about shared areas. The hotel gives La Bocca beach at 1.8 km on foot, the A8 motorway entrance (exit 41) at 1.6 km and the start of the Croisette at 5.9 km.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: for any other hotel in Cannes, ask in writing for the maximum weight, the supplement and the deposit, as these conditions vary a lot between a palace hotel and a simple town-centre address.</div>
      <h2>The Lérins Islands with your dog</h2>
      <p>The Cannes tourist office publishes a "Beaches open to dogs" page for the island of Sainte-Marguerite: dogs are allowed but must be kept on a leash, all year in 2026, with free access and paid transport. A tourist office document lists the coves and beaches concerned, on Sainte-Marguerite as well as Saint-Honorat (Fort beach, Abbey beach, Port beach, among others). For the crossing, guides say dogs on a leash are accepted on the shuttle boats, but no ferry company's own rules have been consulted: check the conditions of the operator you choose. Bring your own poop bags, as the coves have no facilities.</p>
      <h2>On the mainland</h2>
      <p>On the page of the tourist office document that was read, no beach on the Cannes mainland appears among the beaches open to dogs, while beaches in neighbouring towns do (Cagnes-sur-Mer, for example). Several guides report that three Cannes beaches open to dogs in winter, from 1 November to 1 May, but this relies on the municipal order, which has not been consulted: read it or call the town hall before you go.</p>
